      ),
      ProseSection(
        'The Great Widening of the Modern Era',
        'In the nineteenth and twentieth centuries the ratio widened '
            'greatly. After silver was demonetized and no metal backed '
            'money any longer, silver was above all an industrial metal, '
            'while gold remained a store of value. In this span the ratio '
            'has since often lain somewhere between fifty and ninety to '
            'one.\n\n'
            'This span is a rough order of magnitude, not a value precise '
            'to the day. For a short time the ratio can swing sharply: in '
            'mid-January 1980 the Hunt brothers drove the silver price to '
            'nearly fifty dollars per ounce; on "Silver Thursday," March '
            '27, 1980, it then collapsed. Such a spike stood far above the '
            'otherwise usual level.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'How to Read the Ratio in the Curve',
        'The chart in the value calculator does not show the ratio '
            'itself, but the series "silver in gold." That is its '
            'reciprocal: one divided by the gold-silver ratio. This is why '
            'the reading direction reverses.\n\n'
            'If the ratio rises — that is, silver becomes cheaper relative '
            'to gold — then the line "silver in gold" falls. A falling line '
            'below the zero line here means a widening ratio. So one sees '
            'the widening of the modern era as a long descent, not as a '
            'rise. The ancient points stand as individual rings near the '
            'middle, because they are known only as an order of magnitude.',
        illustrationId: 'werte/ratio-curve',
      ),
    ],
