  ProseChapter(
    'Why the Ratio Shifted',
    [
      ProseSection(
        'Bimetallism and Gresham\'s Law',
        'For a long time gold and silver stood side by side as money at '
            'once. This is called bimetallism. So that both circulated as '
            'coins, states fixed a set ratio between them — about sixteen '
            'to one.\n\n'
            'When the free market price diverged from this fixed coin '
            'value, the following happened: the metal that was undervalued '
            'in the coin was worth more as raw metal. People held it back '
            'or melted it down, and what stayed in circulation was the '
            'overvalued one. That is Gresham\'s law — bad money drives out '
            'good. It showed itself as a rule of thumb under fixed coinage '
            'rates.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'The Coinage Acts and the "Crime of 1873"',
        'The United States fixed a ratio of fifteen to one in 1792, '
            'then about sixteen to one in 1834. In 1873 a coinage act '
            'removed silver from free coinage — with this the United States '
            'effectively went over to the gold standard.\n\n'
            'The law was passed and later received the name "Crime of 1873" '
            'from the "Free Silver" movement. Whether a deliberate '
            'conspiracy against silver stood behind it, contemporaries saw '
            'differently: some a quiet coup, others a technical step in a '
            'development that was already underway.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Silver Becomes an Industrial Metal, Gold Stays Money',
        'In the twentieth century the roles of the two metals parted '
            'ways. Silver was needed above all in industry — in '
            'photography, electrics, and later electronics. Its price thus '
            'increasingly hung on the supply and demand of technology.\n\n'
            'Gold, by contrast, remained primarily a store of value and a '
            'reserve of the central banks. What counts as money and what as '
            'a material shapes the price. These diverging roles are one of '
            'the reasons why the ratio widened so far.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Production and Occurrence in the Earth\'s Crust',
        'In the earth\'s crust silver is markedly more abundant than '
            'gold — roughly fifteen to twenty times as much, depending on '
            'the estimate. More silver than gold is also mined year after '
            'year. These figures are rough geological orders of magnitude, '
            'not fixed values.\n\n'
            'One might think that abundance alone sets the monetary ratio. '
            'It is not that simple. How expensive a metal is also depends '
            'on how hard it is to win, what it is needed for, and what role '
            'it plays in the monetary system. Geological abundance is only '
            'one ingredient among several.',
        illustrationId: 'werte/crust-vs-money',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Several Causes at Once',
        'No single cause explains the shift. Coinage acts, Gresham\'s '
            'law, silver\'s new role in industry, finds and production all '
            'worked together. The figures named — coinage rates, '
            'production, abundance — are orders of magnitude.\n\n'
            'Around the "Crime of 1873" the interpretations diverge as soon '
            'as they reach beyond the documented law: some read it as a '
            'conspiracy against silver, others as a sober administrative '
            'act.',
      ),
    ],
