  ProseChapter(
    'The Ratio as a Strategy and the Myth of the Natural Number',
    [
      ProseSection(
        'Mean Reversion: a Strategy',
        'Some investors assume that the gold-silver ratio returns over '
            'the long run to a mean value. If the ratio is high, they buy '
            'silver; if it is low, they buy gold. This idea is called "mean '
            'reversion," the return to the mean.\n\n'
            'A ratio that fluctuated by ten in one century may swing '
            'somewhere entirely different in another. Where some see a '
            'stable mean value to which the ratio finds its way back, '
            'others see only a series of numbers without a fixed anchor.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Crustal Abundance versus the Monetary Ratio',
        'That silver is far more abundant in the earth\'s crust than '
            'gold is explained in the chapter "Why the Ratio Shifted." But '
            'no "natural" monetary ratio follows from abundance in the '
            'crust: abundance in the rock and the price in the monetary '
            'system are two different things.\n\n'
            'The price additionally hangs on production costs, industrial '
            'demand, monetary orders, and trust. Geological abundance is '
            'only one ingredient among several.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Is 16 (or 12) a "Natural" Number?',
        'One hears that the ratio is "by nature" sixteen to one or '
            'twelve to one. The round numbers came out of coinage acts — '
            'they were political and practical settings.\n\n'
            'That troy weight and old coin denominations are built '
            'dozenally is documented and stands in the unit theory. Twelve '
            'was useful in dividing. From there, however, no path leads to '
            'a fixed value ratio of gold to silver: the coinage ordinance '
            'set the number, nature did not prescribe it.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'What the Ratio Means — and What It Does Not',
        'The ratio is a useful measure for comparing gold and silver, '
            'and a piece of economic history. It is not a hidden law of '
            'nature and not a guide to the future.\n\n'
            'Some say the silver market is deliberately suppressed; others '
            'see in it only the ordinary play of supply and demand. Fixed '
            'ratios between paper and physical metal, too, as they are '
            'sometimes cited, diverge widely depending on the source. '
            'Whoever reads the ratio reads history and the present.',
      ),
    ],
