  ProseChapter(
    'Silver Through History',
    [
      ProseSection(
        'The People\'s Metal, Not the King\'s Metal',
        'Gold was the metal of kings and of treasure hoards. Silver was '
            'the metal of everyday money. Whoever bought and sold in daily '
            'life paid, over thousands of years, mostly in silver, not in '
            'gold.\n\n'
            'The reason is simple: silver is rarer than ordinary metals, '
            'but more common than gold. There was enough of it for many '
            'hands and many coins. So it became the metal of the '
            'marketplace — valuable enough to count, and yet widespread '
            'enough to circulate.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Money Means Silver — argent, plata',
        'In several languages, "money" is simply the word for silver. '
            'In French, argent means both silver and money. In Spanish, '
            'plata stands for both. In other languages too, the two terms '
            'coincide.\n\n'
            'But this is no law. The English "money" goes back through '
            'Latin to moneta, the mint; the German "Geld" comes from the '
            'Old High German "gelt," the payment. Neither comes from '
            'silver. The equation "money = silver" is a striking linguistic '
            'cluster, not a rule that holds everywhere.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'From Lydia to the Athenian Owl Tetradrachm',
        'The first coins arose around the sixth century before our era '
            'in Lydia, at first from electrum, a gold-silver alloy. Pure '
            'silver coins soon followed.\n\n'
            'The Athenian tetradrachm with its owl became famous. It was a '
            'heavy silver coin and was accepted across the whole eastern '
            'Mediterranean as reliable money. Whoever took an owl trusted '
            'in the weight and fineness of the silver.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Laurion, Potosí, and the Silver Floods',
        'Great silver always came from great mines. Athens drew its '
            'silver from the pits of Laurion and used it to finance its '
            'fleet as well. Later, Spanish America delivered vast '
            'quantities — above all the mountain of Potosí in what is now '
            'Bolivia.\n\n'
            'These silver floods changed the world: American silver flowed '
            'across Europe all the way to China and shaped the trade of '
            'whole centuries. The output figures for Laurion and Potosí '
            'survive only as estimates, and the sources differ sharply from '
            'one another. One knows the order of magnitude, not the exact '
            'number.',
      ),
    ],
