  ProseChapter(
    'Silver as a Material',
    [
      ProseSection(
        'The Best Conductor There Is',
        'Silver is not only beautiful. It is also an extraordinary '
            'material. Of all metals, silver conducts electric current the '
            'best and heat the best. No other metal is better here.\n\n'
            'On top of that comes the highest sheen: freshly polished '
            'silver throws back more of the visible light than any other '
            'metal. That is why it has sat behind mirrors and reflectors '
            'since time immemorial. These properties make it valuable to '
            'technology, entirely apart from jewelry.',
        illustrationId: 'werte/conductivity',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Electronics, Contacts, and Solder Joints',
        'Because silver conducts so well, it is found in many '
            'electrical components. Switches and relays carry silver '
            'contacts, because they still conduct cleanly even after many '
            'switching cycles. Conductive pastes and solder joints use '
            'silver to carry current reliably.\n\n'
            'Often it is a matter of small amounts per component. In total, '
            'however, electronics is a large consumer. Much of this is '
            'finely distributed and can scarcely be recovered later.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Solar Cells and the Energy-Transition Demand',
        'A growing share of silver goes into solar cells. The fine '
            'conductive tracks on the front of many cells consist of silver '
            'paste; they carry off the generated current. With the '
            'expansion of photovoltaics, this demand grows.\n\n'
            'At the same time, the industry is working to use less silver '
            'per cell. This "thrifting" — the more sparing use per unit — '
            'runs counter to the rising number of units. How the two '
            'finally balance out is an open question.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Antimicrobial and in Medicine',
        'Silver works against germs. Early on, people placed silver in '
            'water or used silver vessels to make it keep. Today, medicine '
            'uses this effect deliberately: in wound dressings, for '
            'instance, and in coatings that inhibit the growth of '
            'bacteria.\n\n'
            'The antimicrobial effect is well documented. It is one more '
            'reason why silver is not only an investment metal, but a used '
            'material with real benefit.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Electric Cars, Robotics, and Future Demand',
        'More electronics usually means more silver. Electric cars '
            'contain numerous electrical contacts, and robotics and '
            'networked devices also need conductive components. From this, '
            'some derive a sharply rising demand.\n\n'
            'The direction — toward more industrial demand — is plausible. '
            'But concrete quantity forecasts for the coming years often '
            'come from the industry itself, such as from the Silver '
            'Institute, an industry body; fixed tonnages for the future are '
            'estimates.',
      ),
    ],
