  ProseChapter(
    'Supply and Demand: Industry versus Investment',
    [
      ProseSection(
        'Consumed Rather Than Hoarded',
        'Silver has two faces. It is an investment metal like gold, but '
            'at the same time an industrial metal that is consumed. A large '
            'part of the annual demand goes into technology — into '
            'electronics, solar cells, and many small applications.\n\n'
            'Herein lies a difference from gold. Gold is hoarded almost '
            'exclusively and scarcely consumed; most of the gold ever mined '
            'still lies somewhere. Silver, by contrast, is used up to a '
            'good extent — and used silver is often finely distributed.',
        illustrationId: 'werte/demand-split',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Most Silver Is a By-Product',
        'Most silver arises as a by-product in the mining of lead, '
            'zinc, and copper — how this happens is set out in the chapter '
            'on mining and production. For this chapter, what matters is '
            'the consequence.\n\n'
            'The silver supply often depends more on the demand for these '
            'main metals than on the silver price itself. A high silver '
            'price alone does not readily bring more silver out of the '
            'ground if the main metals are not in demand.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Above Ground, Below Ground: Stocks and Recycling',
        'Silver lies in three places: still in the ground, in bars and '
            'coins as a stock above ground, and finely distributed in used '
            'products. Bars and coins can easily be melted down again. The '
            'finely distributed silver in electronics and coatings is '
            'harder to recover.\n\n'
            'Recycling therefore covers only part of the demand. How large '
            'the above-ground stocks are exactly, and what share ever '
            'returns, can only be estimated.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Why the Two Demands Pull on the Same Price',
        'Industry and investors buy the same metal on the same market. '
            'Industry needs silver because it conducts and shines. '
            'Investors buy it as a store of value. Both pull on the same '
            'price.\n\n'
            'That makes silver restless. Sometimes technology drives it, '
            'sometimes investment. If both coincide, the price can swing '
            'sharply — more strongly than gold, which is carried almost '
            'exclusively by investment.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'How Much Is Known Exactly',
        'The exact shares fluctuate from year to year. How much silver '
            'goes into industry and how much arises as a by-product is '
            'known only as an order of magnitude, not as a fixed '
            'number.\n\n'
            'Finely distributed applications are poorly recycled. Whether '
            'the consumed industrial silver is thereby "gone forever" and '
            '"scarcely anything" remains above ground, the experts see '
            'differently: some hold the loss to be decisive, the others '
            'point to the large stocks of bars and coins, which can be '
            'melted down again at any time.',
      ),
    ],
