  ProseChapter(
    'Silver as an Investment: "The Poor Man\'s Gold"',
    [
      ProseSection(
        'Cheaper per piece, wilder in its swings',
        'Silver costs far less per ounce than gold. That is why it can '
            'be bought in small steps — a single coin is affordable. This '
            'earned silver the old saying about the "poor man\'s gold."\n\n'
            'The low price per piece comes paired with a more violent up '
            'and down. Historically, silver swings more sharply than gold. '
            'Whoever holds silver has to endure larger swings both upward '
            'and downward.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Half money, half raw material',
        'Silver sits between two worlds. Like gold, it is an old '
            'monetary metal and a store of value. Like copper, it is an '
            'industrial metal that gets consumed.\n\n'
            'This dual nature shapes its price. In crises some buy it like '
            'gold, as a safe haven. When the economy runs hot, industrial '
            'demand pulls. Silver obeys both forces at once.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Why silver exaggerates gold',
        'In many phases silver moves in the same direction as gold, but '
            'more strongly. When gold rises, silver usually gains more '
            'strongly; when gold falls, silver sinks deeper. People say '
            'silver "exaggerates" the movement of gold.\n\n'
            'One reason is the small market: even modest purchases or sales '
            'move the silver price noticeably. The added pull of industry '
            'amplifies the swings.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Coins, bars, and paper',
        'Silver can be held in several ways. Coins and bars are the '
            'metal itself, to touch. Alongside them are paper forms — funds '
            'and futures contracts — that track silver without your having '
            'it in the cupboard.\n\n'
            'Physical silver is bulkier and, in relation to its value, more '
            'expensive to store than gold, because you need a multiple of '
            'the mass for the same value. Paper forms are handier, but '
            'bring their own questions — more on this in the chapter "Paper '
            'versus Metal: The Silver Puzzle."\n\n'
            'Some consider silver undervalued and expect it to catch up to '
            'gold — especially that the gold-silver ratio will return to '
            'the old mark near sixteen. That this ratio is not a natural '
            'constant is explained in the chapters on the gold-silver '
            'ratio.',
      ),
    ],
