  ProseChapter(
    'Paper versus Metal: The Silver Puzzle',
    [
      ProseSection(
        'What a futures market actually is',
        'On a futures market one does not trade the metal itself, but '
            'contracts about future delivery. A futures contract is the '
            'promise to deliver or take a certain quantity of silver at a '
            'later date for a fixed price.\n\n'
            'What matters is this: the vast majority of contracts are '
            'closed out before the delivery date. Buyer and seller settle '
            'their position with an offsetting trade, and no metal ever '
            'flows. The futures market serves above all for price hedging '
            'and trading, not for delivery.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'The claim: much paper, little metal',
        'From this design follows an often-heard claim: that there is '
            'far more silver "on paper" than there is metal truly '
            'deliverable in the warehouses. The open contracts exceed the '
            'immediately available stocks many times over.\n\n'
            'From this it is concluded that the market sits on promises it '
            'cannot redeem, and that a rush on the metal must blow it '
            'apart.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'How much metal lies behind the contracts',
        'The open contracts can exceed the immediately deliverable '
            'registered stocks. For a futures market this is the normal '
            'case, because almost all contracts are closed out before '
            'delivery; metal never has to be there for everyone at '
            'once.\n\n'
            'Some read in this the design of a working market, in which '
            'prices are traded and not bars moved. Others read in it a '
            'promise that breaks in an emergency. A fixed ratio of '
            '"so-and-so-much paper per ounce of metal" cannot be put in '
            'figures for the second reading.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'The Hunt brothers in 1980: a real episode',
        'That the silver market can grow tight is shown by a documented '
            'case. Around 1979 and 1980 the Hunt brothers bought enormous '
            'quantities of silver and contracts and drove the price upward '
            '— to nearly 50 dollars per ounce.\n\n'
            'On "Silver Thursday," the 27th of March 1980, the price '
            'collapsed when the rules were tightened and the Hunts could no '
            'longer finance their purchases. That is a documented episode — '
            'the attempt to dominate a market, and its failure.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'The "Silver Squeeze" of 2021',
        'At the start of 2021 investors on social networks called on '
            'each other to buy silver together and thus trigger a "squeeze" '
            '— in the orbit of the GameStop episode. The silver price rose '
            'briefly.\n\n'
            'No lasting shortage came of it. Some saw in it proof that the '
            'warehouses are more thinly stocked than thought; others saw '
            'above all how quickly such narratives spread without changing '
            'much about the metal.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Manipulation and the alleged ratio',
        'There are documented individual cases of dishonest trading. '
            'Regulators such as the CFTC have penalized individual banks '
            'for "spoofing" — sham orders meant to deceive. These are '
            'documented individual cases.\n\n'
            'On what follows from this, the readings diverge. Some see in '
            'it the tip of an iceberg: the silver price suppressed in '
            'coordinated fashion, a fixed paper-to-metal ratio that forces '
            'the crash, warehouses that are empty and pledged several times '
            'over. Others see penalized individual cases, from which no '
            'lasting, deliberate suppression of "the market" follows.',
      ),
