  ProseChapter(
    'Does Gold Hold Its Value?',
    [
      ProseSection(
        'The question',
        'Gold counts as a store of value. But does it really hold its '
            'value? The question is tricky, for one cannot measure gold in '
            'money without going in circles. Money itself fluctuates — that '
            'is precisely the reason one searches for a fixed value at '
            'all.\n\n'
            'That is why a yardstick outside of money is needed. One '
            'measures gold in something that people needed at all times and '
            'that can be compared across millennia: in food, concretely in '
            'grain. How much bread-corn does a certain quantity of gold '
            'buy? That is the honest question behind the claim of the store '
            'of value.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Why grain in particular is the yardstick',
        'Grain is humanity\'s oldest and most even need. Whoever lives '
            'must eat, and over most of history eating meant above all '
            'bread-corn — wheat, barley, rice. A bushel of grain two '
            'thousand years ago was roughly the same as today: a meal, a '
            'day\'s ration, a piece of survival.\n\n'
            'This constancy makes grain a better yardstick than any '
            'currency. Coins were debased, empires passed away, paper money '
            'came and went — hunger stayed the same. A measure anchored to '
            'an unchanging human need lies less than a measure that hangs '
            'on a government.\n\n'
            'The grain is not entirely neutral either: crop failures, new '
            'cultivation methods, and world trade have moved the grain '
            'price itself. But these fluctuations are small against the '
            'collapse of some currencies. As a "ruler" for very long '
            'stretches of time, grain is therefore more honest than money — '
            'not perfect, but the best available.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Roughly stable over very long time',
        'Over very long stretches of time a fixed quantity of gold buys '
            'roughly comparable quantities of grain. "Roughly" is the '
            'important word here: it is about orders of magnitude, not '
            'fixed numbers.\n\n'
            'A few anchors give a feel for the span, each only as a rough '
            'order of magnitude. In Roman Egypt, gold quantities and grain '
            'prices can be compared in papyri; in medieval England, '
            'Clark\'s long price series reach back from the thirteenth '
            'century onward; and today, too, an ounce of gold again buys a '
            'quantity of grain in a similar order of magnitude as back '
            'then. Between these points lay centuries with quite different '
            'conditions.\n\n'
            'The astonishing thing is that the endpoints of such a long '
            'stretch lie so close together. Over two millennia, the '
            'purchasing power of gold in bread-corn stayed in the same '
            'rough order of magnitude — something that can be said of '
            'hardly any currency.',
        illustrationId: 'werte/purchasing-power',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'But not from year to year',
        'In the short term, gold is anything but stable. Its price in '
            'grain fluctuates markedly over years and decades. Seen over a '
            'generation, a fixed quantity of gold can sometimes buy a lot '
            'and sometimes little grain.\n\n'
            'Whoever bought at the wrong moment and sold again at the wrong '
            'one lost in real terms, even though gold is stable "across the '
            'millennia." The long line reassures; the short one can be '
            'harsh. Gold is a store of value across generations, not a '
            'reliable yardstick from one year to the next.\n\n'
            'That is the other, more modern question: does gold protect '
            'against inflation and crisis within a single person\'s '
            'investing life? It is treated separately in the chapter "Gold '
            'as a Hedge Against Inflation and Crisis." This chapter here '
            'deliberately takes the long, millennia-spanning view against '
            'the grain.\n\n'
            'How this long stability is reflected in the chart — a swinging '
            'within the same order of magnitude, with ancient rings instead '
            'of a continuous line — is shown by the chapter "Reading the '
            'Price Curve."',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'How precise the old values are',
        'The old values are rough, regional, and often handed down only '
            'as an order of magnitude. Ancient grain prices fluctuated with '
            'the harvest and the region; a price from the Nile valley says '
            'little about the same day in Britain. From such numbers no '
            'precise curve can be drawn, only a rough line.\n\n'
            'The statement therefore has two halves: over very long time '
            'the purchasing power of gold in bread-corn stays astonishingly '
            'within the same order of magnitude — with large swings in '
            'between.',
      ),
    ],
    sources: [
      Source(
        'Harper, K. — People, Plagues, and Prices in the Roman World (2016)',
        'https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-economic-history',
        'R2',
        'A2',
      ),
      Source(
        'Clark, G. — The Price History of English Agriculture, 1209–1914',
        'https://faculty.econ.ucdavis.edu/faculty/gclark/papers/Agprice.pdf',
        'R2',
        'A2',
      ),
      Source(
