      ),
    ],
  ),
  ProseChapter(
    'The Gold Standard and Its End',
    [
      ProseSection(
        'What a gold peg means',
        'A gold peg means: a country\'s money is legally tied to a '
            'fixed amount of gold. A unit of money stands for so and so '
            'many grams of gold, and in principle you can exchange paper '
            'for metal. This is meant to secure trust, because the state '
            'cannot increase the money supply at will.\n\n'
            'Money thereby becomes a claim on weight — just as money once '
            'was simply weighed metal. What this old connection between '
            'money and weight looked like is set out in the unit theory '
            'under "Currency."',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'The classical gold standard (about 1870 to 1914)',
        'In the decades before the First World War, many countries tied '
            'their money to gold. Exchange rates were thereby largely '
            'fixed, and trade across borders became more predictable. Many '
            'regard this time as a golden age of stable money.\n\n'
            'It was not calm, though. There were banking panics, long '
            'phases of falling prices, and sharp recessions. The fixed gold '
            'peg took a tool out of governments\' hands with which they '
            'could have responded to crises.',
        illustrationId: 'werte/goldstandard-timeline',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Bretton Woods: gold by way of the dollar',
        'After the Second World War, the Bretton Woods agreement '
            'reordered money. It is often said in brief that this was a '
            'gold standard — that foreshortens the matter. Only the US '
            'dollar was tied to gold, at a legal value of 35.00 dollars per '
            'fine ounce, set by the Gold Reserve Act of 1934 (previously '
            '20.67 dollars).\n\n'
            'All other currencies were not tied to gold, but to the dollar. '
            'By way of the dollar they were indirectly attached to gold. '
            'More precisely, then, it is a gold-exchange standard: gold at '
            'the core, the dollar as the link.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'The Nixon shock of 1971',
        'Over time there were far more dollars abroad than gold in the '
            'American vaults, and more and more countries wanted to '
            'exchange their dollars for metal. In 1971, President Nixon '
            'suspended the redemption of dollars for gold. With that, the '
            'last gold peg of money came to an end.\n\n'
            'From then on the dollar was no longer redeemable in gold. The '
            'step was intended as temporary — it has remained permanent.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'What came after: pure paper money',
        'Since then the world\'s money no longer rests on metal. Its '
            'value is based on trust, on the stability of the state, and on '
            'the scarcity that the central bank maintains. Experts call '
            'this fiat money — money that holds because it is supposed to '
            'hold.\n\n'
            'With that, the yardstick itself has become movable. This is '
            'precisely the reason why one prefers to measure the value of '
            'gold against something that people have always needed — '
            'against grain, for instance, as explained in the chapter "Does '
            'Gold Hold Its Value?"',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Breach of promise or orderly end?',
        'The classical gold standard was no age of pure calm — panics, '
            'deflation, and recessions are part of it. And Bretton Woods '
            'was not a full gold standard, but tied only the dollar to '
            'gold.\n\n'
            'What happened in 1971 was seen differently by the camps: to '
            'the hard-money camp it counted as a breach of promise or even '
            'theft, to others as the orderly end of a peg that was no '
            'longer tenable anyway. More dollars flowed out of the country '
            'than there was gold in the vault — that was the pressure '
            'behind it. The values 20.67 and 35.00 dollars per ounce are '
            'historical legal figures, not present-day prices.',
      ),
    ],
    sources: [
      Source(
