  ProseChapter(
    'Central Banks and Their Gold',
    [
      ProseSection(
        'Gold without a gold standard: why still?',
        'Since 1971, no gold has backed money anymore. Even so, the '
            'central banks still hold large amounts of it. At first this '
            'seems contradictory: why hoard gold when it no longer ties the '
            'money at all?\n\n'
            'The reason is no longer legal, but practical. Gold is a '
            'reserve that belongs to no counterparty and cannot default. A '
            'bond is a promise made by someone else; a bar is simply there.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Reserve and diversification',
        'A central bank does not hold its reserves in a single form. '
            'Alongside foreign currencies it holds gold, because it behaves '
            'differently from them and often keeps its value in crises. In '
            'this way it spreads the risk across several baskets.\n\n'
            'Gold has its own role here: it is recognized across borders, '
            'it is tied to no single state, and it remains worth something '
            'even when trust in a currency wanes. This is precisely what '
            'makes it the reserve for the worst case.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Where the gold lies: storage and custody',
        'The central banks\' gold lies in heavily secured vaults. Much '
            'of it rests with a few large custodians, because bars there '
            'can be reassigned between owners quickly and without '
            'transport. A central bank therefore often stores its gold not '
            'only at home, but also abroad.\n\n'
            'This has advantages in trading, but raises a question of '
            'trust: whoever stores abroad must trust the custodian. Out of '
            'this question, public debates have arisen in several '
            'countries.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'The repatriation debates',
        'Several times countries have brought part of their gold stored '
            'abroad back home. The German Bundesbank, for example, moved a '
            'share of its holdings back to Frankfurt and gave a public '
            'account of it.\n\n'
            'Alongside these relocations, the suspicion circulates that the '
            'gold is "not there at all" or has secretly been lent out '
            'several times over. The relocations are documented and backed '
            'by an accounting; for the suspicion, such proof is lacking.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Which country holds how much',
        'Which country holds how much gold, and in what ranking, is '
            'deliberately not stated here — such figures change and grow '
            'stale. What matters here are the lasting reasons: trust, a '
            'crisis reserve, no default of a counterparty.\n\n'
            'Some read the central banks\' gold purchases geopolitically — '
            'as an attempt to weaken a leading currency. Others see in them '
            'simply the old search for a reserve that belongs to no state. '
            'Both readings entwine themselves around the same documented '
            'purchases.',
      ),
    ],
