  ProseChapter(
    'Paper Gold and Physical Gold',
    [
      ProseSection(
        'Holding Metal or Holding a Claim',
        'There are two fundamentally different ways to be in gold. You '
            'either hold the metal itself — bars or coins in your hand or '
            'in a vault — or you hold a piece of paper that certifies a '
            'claim to gold. The one is ownership of metal, the other is a '
            'claim against someone.\n\n'
            'The difference sounds small, but it is the heart of the '
            'matter. With metal you carry no counterparty risk: the bar '
            'stays yours, even if others default. With a claim, everything '
            'depends on the debtor being able to deliver.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Gold ETFs: Allocated and Unallocated',
        'Many investors do not hold gold themselves but through an '
            'exchange-traded fund — an ETF — that stores it for them. Here '
            'allocated parts ways with unallocated. Allocated means: '
            'specific, numbered bars are assigned to the holder; the metal '
            'belongs to him.\n\n'
            'Unallocated means: the holder has a claim against the bank for '
            'a quantity of gold, but no specific bar. As long as everything '
            'runs smoothly, the difference is invisible. If the custodian '
            'fails, it is decisive — the allocated holder has his metal, '
            'the other only a claim.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Futures: COMEX and Delivery',
        'On futures exchanges such as the COMEX, gold contracts are '
            'traded that promise delivery on a later day. Most of these '
            'contracts, however, do not end in metal but are settled in '
            'cash beforehand or rolled forward.\n\n'
            'That most contracts are settled in cash is by design: the '
            'exchange serves many to hedge prices, not to procure bars. '
            'Anyone who really wants metal can demand delivery.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'The London Market (LBMA) and "Loco London" Gold',
        'The largest trade in physical gold runs over-the-counter '
            'through London. There "Loco London" means gold that lies in '
            'the London vaults and changes owner through rebooking among '
            'the members, without being moved. The LBMA sets the standards '
            'for bars and settlement.\n\n'
            'Much of this is unallocated: a credit in gold at a bank, not '
            'specific bars. That makes the trade fast and cheap, but it '
            'shifts the risk onto trust in the custodian — once again the '
            'old separation of claim and metal.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'The Leverage and Coverage Question',
        'Because so many claims are denominated in gold, one asks: does '
            'a bar really stand behind every claim? Unallocated claims can '
            'exceed the physical metal — by how much depends on what you '
            'count. The multiples cited for this diverge widely.\n\n'
            'The practice of rehypothecation exists: the same collateral '
            'can pass through several hands. Whether it follows from this '
            'that London or COMEX gold is sold many times over en masse, '
            'observers see differently: some read into it an inflated '
            'market, others the normal mechanics of securities trading.',
        illustrationId: 'werte/allocated',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'What Is Documented and What Is Claimed',
        'Allocated means ownership of the metal, unallocated a claim '
            'with counterparty risk. Most futures contracts end in cash — '
            'that is part of the exchange\'s design.\n\n'
            'Against this stands the recurring "There is too little '
            'physical gold, the market will collapse." Individual penalties '
            'for "spoofing" — feigned orders — are documented; some derive '
            'from this a permanently suppressed gold price, others see in '
            'it individual punished cases without effect on the long trend.',
      ),
    ],
    sources: [
