  ProseChapter(
    'Where the Metal Comes From: Mining and Production',
    [
      ProseSection(
        'Placer in the River: Placer Gold and the First Gold Rush',
        'In the beginning there was no mine, but the river. Gold is '
            'heavy and does not rust, so it collects as fine grains and '
            'flakes in the sand of streams — the so-called placer deposits. '
            'Whoever swirls the sand in a pan lets the light rock float off '
            'and keeps back the heavy gold.\n\n'
            'This is how the great gold rushes began: California around '
            '1849, Australia, the Klondike. One needed hardly any tools, '
            'only a pan and patience. But the easily reached river gold was '
            'washed out quickly, and the prospectors moved on or dug '
            'deeper.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Deep in the Rock: Hard-Rock Mining and the Cyanide Process',
        'Most gold sits firmly in the rock, often only in tiny amounts '
            'per ton. To win it, the ore is broken, ground fine, and '
            'chemically dissolved out. Since the late 19th century this has '
            'mostly been done with a weak cyanide leach, to which the gold '
            'binds and out of which it is then precipitated.\n\n'
            'The process is effective, but delicate: cyanide is toxic, and '
            'the residues must be safely stored. If a tailings pond breaks, '
            'the leach reaches rivers. Hard-rock mining thus pulls more '
            'metal out of poor ore, but for that it demands large '
            'facilities and careful control.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Silver as a By-Product: The Side Product of Lead and Copper Mines',
        'Silver is rarely mined on its own. The larger part comes as a '
            'by-product — in the smelting of lead, zinc, copper, and gold. '
            'The silver sits in the same ore and is won along with it, when '
            'digging is being done anyway for another metal.\n\n'
            'This has a peculiar consequence: how much silver comes to '
            'market often depends less on the silver price than on the '
            'demand for copper or zinc. That most of the silver is a '
            'by-product is regarded as well documented. The exact share, '
            'however, varies from year to year and from country to country.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Where It Is Mined: The Major Countries and Deposits',
        'Gold is mined on all continents. Among the major producing '
            'countries over the years are China, Australia, Russia, the '
            'United States, Canada, and several countries of Africa and '
            'South America. The ranking shifts, yet the list remains '
            'remarkably stable.\n\n'
            'For silver, Mexico, Peru, China, and some Andean countries '
            'stand far in front — fittingly, since it is mostly won '
            'together with other metals. Exact annual quantities are '
            'deliberately not given here: they change continuously. The '
            'point is orders of magnitude and rankings, not fixed numbers.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Effort and Tailings: Energy, Water, and the Ecological Price',
        'Getting gold out of poor ore costs a great deal. For a single '
            'gram, tons of rock often must be moved, ground, and treated. '
            'This consumes energy and water and leaves behind large amounts '
            'of tailings and sludge.\n\n'
            'Particularly difficult is the small, often unregulated gold '
            'mining in poorer regions. There the gold is in many cases '
            'bound with mercury, which afterwards evaporates or reaches '
            'rivers. The mercury harms people and the environment; the '
            'international Minamata Convention tries to push back its '
            'use.\n\n'
            'Every ounce won has an effort and a trace. Whoever grasps gold '
            'as value holds this price in his hand as well.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        '"Peak Gold" and the Limits of Production',
        'Again and again it is said that the world has reached the peak '
            'of gold production or will soon pass it — so-called "Peak '
            'Gold." Some see production at a permanent limit, others hold '
            'the peak to be not yet reached.\n\n'
            'For what counts as worth mining is not a fixed quantity. If '
            'the price rises or the technology improves, unprofitable rock '
            'becomes a deposit. Range figures for reserves are model '
            'calculations under assumptions, not expiry dates.\n\n'
            'The easily reached deposits are largely found, and new finds '
            'are on average deeper and poorer. Whether and when production '
            'really declines permanently remains open.',
      ),
    ],
