  ProseChapter(
    'Melted Down Again: The Scrap-Metal Recycling Business',
    [
      ProseSection(
        'The Metal Is Never Lost: Why Gold Circulates So Well',
        'Gold does not rust, does not burn, and does not dissolve. What '
            'was once mined is almost always still there — as jewelry, as a '
            'bar, in a vault, or in a device. It can be melted down and '
            'recast without becoming any worse.\n\n'
            'According to a much-cited estimate, nearly all gold ever mined '
            'exists on in some form. It comes, among others, from the World '
            'Gold Council, the industry advocacy group of the gold '
            'business. The order of magnitude holds: gold hardly vanishes, '
            'it only changes its shape.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'The Second Mine: Scrap Jewelry Gold and Investment Bars',
        'Beside the mines there is a second source: scrap gold. Old '
            'pieces of jewelry, discarded chains, melted-down coins, and '
            'bars flow back into the market and become pure metal '
            'again.\n\n'
            'This "second mine" needs no mine and no tailings. In countries '
            'with much gold jewelry it is a significant part of the supply '
            '— above all when a high price moves people to give up old '
            'gold.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Gold in the Phone: Electronic Scrap as a Raw-Material Source',
        'In phones, computers, and circuit boards there are tiny '
            'amounts of gold, silver, and other metals, because they '
            'conduct well and do not corrode. In sum, a great treasure lies '
            'in them — this is called "urban mining," mining in the '
            'city.\n\n'
            'But the treasure is scattered and firmly built in. To raise it '
            'demands collecting, dismantling, and laborious separation. The '
            'world\'s electronic scrap grows quickly, and only a part is '
            'properly recovered. Urban mining is real, but no effortless '
            'money.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Recycling Follows the Price, Not the Demand',
        'How much scrap metal comes back depends strongly on the price. '
            'If it rises, more people give up their old gold, and recycling '
            'springs into action. If it falls, one would rather keep the '
            'piece. Fixed shares therefore cannot be given — the values '
            'vary considerably from year to year.\n\n'
            'Recycling thus follows the market, not the demand of industry. '
            'It is a useful buffer and spares the environment compared with '
            'new mining, but it does not replace it. The bulk of the fresh '
            'supply is still delivered by the mines.',
      ),
    ],
    sources: [
      Source(
