      ),
    ],
  ),
  ProseChapter(
    'Where the Gold Lies: Vaults, Banks, and Free Ports',
    [
      ProseSection(
        'Fortresses of Gold: Fort Knox and the New York Fed\'s Vault',
        'The best-known gold store is probably Fort Knox in Kentucky, '
            'the bullion depository of the United States. Its name has '
            'become almost proverbial for impregnability: a massive vault '
            'building, heavily guarded, on military grounds.\n\n'
            'At least as important is the vault of the Federal Reserve Bank '
            'of New York. It lies deep in the bedrock beneath Manhattan and '
            'holds gold not only for the United States but for many states '
            'and institutions. The bars there are not sold but moved — from '
            'one compartment to another, when one country pays another.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'The London Marketplace: LBMA, "Good Delivery," and the Vaults',
        'London has long been a hub of the gold trade. There the London '
            'Bullion Market Association sets the standard: only bars that '
            'meet the "Good Delivery" norm — checked for weight, fineness, '
            'and origin — are readily accepted in large-scale trading. The '
            'norm makes the metal fungible: one tested bar is as good as '
            'another.\n\n'
            'Behind the market stands a network of vaults in which the '
            'metal is stored. What it means to hold "Loco London" gold '
            'there, and how that differs from owning the metal yourself, is '
            'covered in the chapter "Paper Gold and Physical Gold."',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'The Swiss Hub: Refineries and Free Ports',
        'A surprisingly large share of the world\'s gold passes through '
            'Switzerland. Several of the leading refineries are based '
            'there, melting raw gold and scrap gold into standardized bars '
            '— including the smaller sizes that are in demand in Asia.\n\n'
            'Then there are the free ports: warehouses where goods sit '
            'without being legally "imported" for customs purposes. Gold '
            'can be stored and traded there almost as if it were on neutral '
            'ground. This makes Switzerland a quiet hub of the metal.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Storing for Others: Central-Bank Gold and ETF Custody',
        'A vault often holds metal not for one owner but for many. '
            'Central banks hold gold as a reserve, some in their own '
            'country, some abroad — for instance in New York or London '
            '(more on this, including the repatriation debates, in the '
            'chapter "Central Banks and Their Gold").\n\n'
            'Gold funds (ETFs) also store metal for their investors. A '
            'custodian holds the bars, a trustee keeps the books, and a '
            'share in the fund corresponds to a share in the stored gold. '
            'Thus physical metal becomes a tradable security — and yet '
            'somewhere a real bar still lies.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Stories and Evidence Around the Great Vaults',
        'Stories cluster around the great vaults. The best-known claims '
            'that there is no gold left in Fort Knox at all and that the '
            'holdings are being concealed. There is no evidence for '
            'this.\n\n'
            'In the same atmosphere belongs the rumor that the same bars '
            'are pledged several times over — this is placed in the chapter '
            '"Paper Gold and Physical Gold." The ratios cited for it are '
            'not proven.\n\n'
            'There are official figures and occasional audits for the great '
            'holdings, but no seamless, public, real-time check.',
      ),
    ],
    sources: [
