  ProseChapter(
    'Platinum and Palladium: Noble, but Industrial',
    [
      ProseSection(
        'The Platinum Group: Six Rare Relatives',
        'Besides gold and silver, the Werterechner knows two more '
            'precious metals: platinum and palladium. Both belong to the '
            'platinum group, a family of six chemically related metals. '
            'They are very rare, very durable, and hard to melt.\n\n'
            'In Europe, platinum became known only in the 18th century. The '
            'peoples of the Andes, however, had been working it long before '
            'that. The Spanish conquerors called it disdainfully "platina," '
            'that is "little silver," because it was so hard to work and '
            'fouled their gold sand.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'The Heart of the Catalytic Converter: Why the Car Sets the Price',
        'Unlike gold and silver, platinum and palladium are above all '
            'industrial metals. Their most important use is the catalytic '
            'converter in cars: a thin layer of the metal turns toxic '
            'exhaust gases into more harmless substances.\n\n'
            'So their price moves less with investors and jewelry than with '
            'the car industry. When many cars sell, demand rises; when the '
            'market stalls, it falls. This makes these metals far more '
            'dependent on the business cycle than gold.',
        illustrationId: 'werte/catalyst',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Diesel versus Petrol: Why Platinum and Palladium Can Swap',
        'In catalytic converters, platinum and palladium can be partly '
            'substituted for one another. Roughly speaking: diesel engines '
            'tend to need platinum, petrol engines tend to need palladium. '
            'When the market shifts from one type of drive to the other, '
            'demand shifts too.\n\n'
            'That is why first one metal and then the other has been the '
            'more expensive. Anyone who thinks platinum is "by nature" more '
            'valuable than palladium is mistaken: the ratio has already '
            'reversed several times. But the substitution has technical '
            'limits — the two are not fully replaceable.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'The Hydrogen Bet: Fuel Cells and Electrolyzers',
        'Platinum is also found in fuel cells and in devices that split '
            'water into hydrogen. If transport one day relies more heavily '
            'on hydrogen, that could create a new, large source of '
            'demand.\n\n'
            'Whether and how quickly the hydrogen economy arrives is an '
            'open question — and with it, how large the additional platinum '
            'demand would turn out to be.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Precious Metal and Raw Material at Once',
        'Platinum and palladium are both at once: noble stores of value '
            'and industrial raw materials. That makes them harder to assess '
            'than gold. Their price depends more strongly on technology, '
            'regulations, and the business cycle.\n\n'
            'On top of that: their production is concentrated in a few '
            'regions. That explains why these two metals can swing more '
            'sharply than gold, which is mined more broadly.',
      ),
    ],
