  ProseChapter(
    'Should Money Return to Gold?',
    [
      ProseSection(
        'The Argument for the Peg',
        'Those who favor a gold peg value above all the discipline. If '
            'money is tied to a gold reserve, the state cannot expand it at '
            'will. This is meant to guard against inflation and give money '
            'a firm anchor.\n\n'
            'The long stability also speaks for gold in these eyes: over '
            'very long periods it has roughly held its purchasing power, as '
            'the chapter on grain shows. Money anchored in this way, they '
            'argue, is more honest than money that can be multiplied.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'The Argument Against',
        'The other side considers the peg dangerously rigid. If the '
            'money supply is chained to gold, a government cannot '
            'counteract a crisis. Many hold exactly this responsible for '
            'the depth of the great economic crisis around 1930 — money '
            'stayed scarce when it needed to grow.\n\n'
            'Moreover, a system tied to gold tends toward deflation: if the '
            'economy grows faster than the gold reserve, prices must fall. '
            'Falling prices sound good, but they often paralyze spending '
            'and employment.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Why No Major Country Has Returned',
        'Despite all its advocates, no major country has returned to '
            'the gold standard. If you ask economic experts, the majority '
            'reject a return.\n\n'
            'The practical reason is flexibility: today\'s states want to '
            'be able to steer their money supply in order to cushion '
            'crises. A gold peg narrows that room to maneuver.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Gold as an Anchor Beside Money, Not as Money',
        'Between "all gold" and "no gold" lies a middle way that many '
            'states actually take: they hold large gold reserves as a '
            'cushion but do not tie their money to them. Gold thus serves '
            'as an anchor beside money, not as the money itself.\n\n'
            'In this role, gold is undisputedly important today. On the '
            'sharper question — bringing gold back as the money — the '
            'experts continue to argue.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'An Open Question',
        'This is an open question of economic policy. Some expect that '
            'gold will one day again back or replace state money; others '
            'see it lastingly as a reserve beside money, not as the money '
            'itself.\n\n'
            'A ratio like sixteen or twelve to one is no law of nature '
            'here. Such numbers came out of coinage acts, as the chapter on '
            'the gold-silver ratio explains.',
      ),
    ],
    sources: [
