      ),
    ],
  ),
  ProseChapter(
    'Gold and Bitcoin: "Digital Gold"?',
    [
      ProseSection(
        'Where the image of "digital gold" comes from',
        'Bitcoin is often called "digital gold." The comparison is '
            'natural, because both are supposed to be scarce stores of '
            'value that no state can multiply at will. The image is catchy '
            '— and it holds up only in part.\n\n'
            'The following sections compare where Bitcoin and gold resemble '
            'each other and where they do not.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Where the comparison holds: scarcity',
        'On the question of scarcity, the image fits best. Gold is rare '
            'because it is rare in the earth. Bitcoin is scarce because its '
            'rules cap the total supply firmly at twenty-one million units '
            '— that is a verifiable property of the protocol.\n\n'
            'But programmed scarcity is not the same as natural rarity. '
            'Gold\'s limit is set by geology, which no one can change. '
            'Bitcoin\'s limit is set by a rule that people have written — '
            'firm, but man-made.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Where it falls short: millennia versus decades',
        'The biggest difference is time. Gold has been money and a '
            'store of value for millennia; over very long stretches it '
            'roughly held its purchasing power. Bitcoin, by contrast, has '
            'only existed since 2009.\n\n'
            'How something has fared over three millennia says more about '
            'its durability than the short time Bitcoin has existed so far. '
            'Bitcoin is also documented as highly volatile and '
            'energy-intensive. Whether it holds its value across '
            'generations is simply not yet tested.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Tangible versus purely digital',
        'Gold is a substance you can weigh, test, and hold in your hand '
            '— which is why it fits the troy weights of the value '
            'calculator. Bitcoin has no body; it is an entry in a '
            'distributed ledger.\n\n'
            'Both have two sides. Gold has to be stored and secured, but in '
            'return it endures without electricity or a network. Bitcoin '
            'can be sent across the world effortlessly, but in return it '
            'depends on computers and power grids.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'An open debate',
        'Some say gold will soon be displaced by state money or by '
            'crypto. Others consider Bitcoin a bubble and gold '
            'irreplaceable.\n\n'
            'The value calculator knows gold, silver, platinum, and '
            'palladium, because these are substances with a long, '
            'documented history. Whether "digital gold" will one day share '
            'that heritage, the future will show.',
      ),
    ],
    sources: [
      Source(
