      ),
    ],
  ),
  ProseChapter(
    'Reading the Price Curve',
    [
      ProseSection(
        'The three series',
        'The chart shows three series, and all three measure against '
            'gold. "Silver in gold" tells you what a weight of silver is '
            'worth in gold. "Grain in gold" tells you what a quantity of '
            'grain costs in gold — that is, how much breadgrain you get for '
            'your gold. "Gold in grain" is its reciprocal, derived from the '
            'same series: what a piece of gold buys in grain.\n\n'
            'Each series tells a different story. Silver against gold is a '
            'question of the two monetary metals relative to each other. '
            'Grain against gold is the purchasing power of gold measured '
            'against the staple food — how far this thread carries through '
            'the centuries is set out in the chapter "Does Gold Hold Its '
            'Value?". Here it is only about how to read the lines in the '
            'picture.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'The zero line in the middle',
        'Each series is referenced to its oldest known value. This '
            'oldest value is set to one and lies as a bold zero line '
            'straight across the middle. Everything later is measured '
            'against it.\n\n'
            'The reading direction is simple: above the line means more '
            'expensive relative to gold, below it cheaper. If a line rises, '
            'the thing has become more valuable relative to gold; if it '
            'falls, it has become cheaper relative to gold. Because the '
            'middle is the starting point, you can see at a glance whether '
            'things have moved upward or downward since then.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'The reversal in "silver in gold"',
        'With the silver series, one twist must be kept in mind. The '
            'chart does not show the familiar gold-silver ratio, but its '
            'reciprocal: silver measured in gold. That is why a falling '
            'line here stands for a widening ratio.\n\n'
            'When silver becomes cheaper relative to gold, the usual ratio '
            'number grows — and the "silver in gold" line sinks. The great '
            'spreading of modern times therefore appears in the picture as '
            'a long decline, not as a rise. The long history of this ratio '
            'itself belongs in the chapters on the gold-silver ratio; here '
            'it only matters how it shows up in the curve.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Ancient rings and the uncertainty band',
        'The ancient points look different from the modern line. They '
            'stand as individual rings, not connected, and each carries an '
            'estimated band from a low to a high value. The band is wide '
            'because ancient prices were irregular, varied by region, and '
            'are known only as a rough estimate.\n\n'
            'That no line runs across the gaps is deliberate. Between a '
            'Babylonian and a Roman point lie centuries without a reliable '
            'figure. A continuous line would feign a course there that no '
            'one knows. The modern series, by contrast, is dense enough to '
            'be drawn as a line.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Pan, zoom, fit',
        'The curve opens via the "Curve" button, which replaces the '
            'keypad; the × symbol closes it again. By default the picture '
            'shows roughly the last hundred years — the stretch with the '
            'densest data.\n\n'
            'With your finger you can pan the view, and with two fingers '
            'you can zoom. Whoever zooms all the way out — using the fit '
            'button or a double tap — sees the full span back into '
            'antiquity, with the oldest value on the left near the zero '
            'line. The values on the axis are factors relative to this '
            'starting value and follow the chosen number base, dozenal or '
            'decimal.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Data and marks',
        'The curve is a rough, documented reconstruction from cited '
            'sources — compiled in two passes by researching and verifying '
            'helpers. The source line in the chart lists them.\n\n'
            'The modern line rests on dense data, the ancient rings on '
            'rough marks. For the oldest points only rough estimates exist, '
            'no day-by-day values. The picture shows a long movement.',
      ),
    ],
