  ProseChapter(
    'Contested Interpretations',
    [
      ProseSection(
        'Where the evidence ends',
        'Up to here it was about weights, substances, history, and '
            'prices. This chapter gathers the claims and interpretations '
            'around money and metal that belong in a text about value.\n\n'
            'Some of these threads have by now received a chapter of their '
            'own that treats them in detail; here they stand as open '
            'questions.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Sound money',
        'Some consider metal-backed money the most honest, because it '
            'cannot be multiplied at will. Others counter that a fixed '
            'money supply can strangle an economy. Both sides have '
            'arguments.\n\n'
            'Whether money should return to gold is laid out with both '
            'sides in the chapter "Should Money Return to Gold?" and is not '
            'argued again here.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Intrinsic value',
        'Does gold have an "intrinsic" value, or does it count only '
            'because everyone has agreed on it? Philosophers and economists '
            'have argued over this for a long time. Precious metals have a '
            'use value in jewelry and industry, but their monetary value '
            'rests largely on agreement.\n\n'
            'The older notion that a thing carries its value within itself '
            'is regarded as outdated today; most economists see value as '
            'something that arises only in exchange.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Twelve in money',
        'That troy weight and the old £sd denomination are built '
            'dozenally is documented. The leap from there to "twelve is the '
            'natural basis of money" is not. Twelve helped with dividing '
            '(by 2, 3, 4, and 6), but alongside it stood the twenty of the '
            'shillings and the sixteen of avoirdupois. More on this is '
            'found in the foundations of the theory.\n\n'
            'Still, the thought is appealing: a measure that can be cleanly '
            'thirded and quartered suits money well, which has to be '
            'divided up. Twelve was a clever choice for money, just as it '
            'was for measuring.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'Manipulation and the supposed ratio',
        'Around silver and gold persistent tales circulate: that the '
            'price is being deliberately suppressed, that the vaults are '
            'empty, that a fixed paper-to-metal ratio forces the crash. '
            'Individual penalties for "spoofing" — faked orders — are '
            'documented; a lasting, coordinated suppression of the price is '
            'not.\n\n'
            'These threads, with their documented cores, are placed in the '
            'chapters "Paper versus Metal" and "Paper Gold and Physical '
            'Gold." The documented individual cases are one thing, the '
            'conspiracy interpretation another.\n\n'
            'Some expect the gold-silver ratio to return to an old mark '
            'near sixteen; others counter that this number is no law of '
            'nature — that is in the chapters on the ratio.',
      ),
      ProseSection(
        'What remains open',
        'In the end there is less certainty than some expect. What a '
            'thing is "really worth" cannot be measured like its weight. '
            'Value arises between people, in scarcity, use, and trust — and '
            'all of that shifts over time. A calculator can convert '
            'quantities; whether they hold their value, it cannot '
            'promise.\n\n'
            'Money itself also remains an open question. Whether it should '
            'be tied to metal, whether "digital gold" inherits from old '
            'gold, whether silver catches up to gold — these debates are '
            'fanned out in their own chapters ("Should Money Return to '
            'Gold?", "Gold and Bitcoin"), and none is settled.\n\n'
            'Whoever understands the value of gold and silver thereby also '
            'understands the limits of what can be known about value at '
            'all.',
      ),
    ],
